WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.259 --> 00:00:05.799
This is the Technical Difficulties,
we’re playing Citation Needed.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.720
Joining me today: he reads books, y'know,
it’s Chris Joel.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:08.720
“Hello!”

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.880
Everybody’s favourite Gary Brannan,
Gary Brannan.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:16.039
♫ I-i-if women like that like men like those,
then why don’t women like me? ♫

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:19.640
Singing(!)

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.730
And the bounciest man on the internet,
Matt Gray.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:22.730
Howdy, YouTube!

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:24.720
- Oh!
- Nice. Nice.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.180
In front of me I’ve got an article from Wikipedia
and these folks can’t see it.

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:31.840
Every fact they get right is
a point and a ding

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.350
and there's a special prize for particularly
good answers which is...

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.219
And today we are talking about John Stonehouse.

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:44.390
Did he live in a greenhouse?

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:46.420
Completely against type.

00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:49.040
Now's when you ding, Tom(!)

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.940
No. I can’t give you that.

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:53.399
Am I close?

00:00:53.399 --> 00:00:54.399
No...

00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:55.399
In any way?

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.399
No.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:58.460
Was he... a bricklayer?

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:00.739
Stone House? No. No he wasn’t.

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:04.040
He did go to work in a House, and there’s
a capital H in that.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:05.440
A House of something.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:06.440
Cards.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:07.440
Fun.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:08.440
Ill repute.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:09.980
I mean, you’re pretty close with
House of Cards.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:10.980
Parliament.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:11.980
Yes, have a point.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:12.980
- Oh!
- Yay.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:16.870
Born in Southampton, educated there and at
the London School of Economics.

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:18.020
So was he not a politiciser?

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:24.430
And then he went into politics, as the
Labour Cooperative Member of Parliament.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.940
So he owned some small supermarkets(?)

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.100
Erm, sort of...

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.980
Did you hear that? Clunk-clunk-clunk-clunk!

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:34.920
“Archivist gear engaged.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.130
“Everybody else shut up, I know the answer.”

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.040
I don’t! But you do still have
Cooperative Labour MPs.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.040
In what sense?

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.460
They don’t sit at the back going,
“F*** no.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:47.520
“It’s bollocks, we’re not doing it.”.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:48.700
Pretty much.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.320
He worked very closely to the
Foreign Office.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:56.780
There was the Foreign Office, the Home Office,
the India Office and then another one.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.060
Commonwealth.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:58.900
Ooh, no…

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.660
No, it’s pre-Commonwealth. Empire?

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.420
Halfway between the two.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.140
Com-pire.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:06.930
Places We Stole Office.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:13.010
Yes, it’s the Colonial Office, so yes, I will
give you the point for that, absolutely right.

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:17.510
Yes, he became Minister for State for Technology
and then took a role…

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.800
Was he going around people’s houses trying
to sell cable?

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.060
You never had that?

00:02:25.069 --> 00:02:26.889
Not from the Colonies Office, no!

00:02:26.889 --> 00:02:29.159
He moved on from there, this was...

00:02:29.159 --> 00:02:32.750
You said technology, I don't for...

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:37.110
I know nothing about politics or other countries!
I've got to go somewhere.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:38.110
I’ve just realised that...

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:39.870
Would you like some Colonial Cable?

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:42.959
I’ve just realised, we’ve not actually
touched on telegraphs yet

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:45.540
and we have to do that at least once or twice
a seas…

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:46.700
Oh, f*** off!

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:54.460
Yes, he became Minister for State for Technology,
but that meant he then transitioned into what?

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:59.760
And this was in charge of telecommunications
as well as telegraphy, everything like that.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:00.950
- Post Office.
- Postmaster General.

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:05.080
You’re both getting the point. He was the
Postmaster General of the United Kingdom.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.019
Hell of a title, that.

00:03:07.019 --> 00:03:11.329
Yes. He was also the last Postmaster General
of the United Kingdom.

00:03:11.329 --> 00:03:12.799
Oh God, what did he do?

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.360
Nothing, they’ve just all been
really specific since.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:17.160
(Yes!)

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.040
Sorry, Matt!

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:18.900
Specific...

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:19.860
Oh…

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.919
Anything that has General on the end
sounds great, anyway.

00:03:24.919 --> 00:03:27.650
He oversaw the jamming of something.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:29.370
A marmalade factory.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:33.859
There’s this sort-of jam war going on where
they just…

00:03:33.859 --> 00:03:35.739
He’s holding toast at the other end.

00:03:35.739 --> 00:03:38.450
That’s Wallace and Gromit, isn’t it, actually?

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:40.480
We must stop this terrible conflict...

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:42.540
to preserve life.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.540
Ohhh!

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:50.099
Even the audience was
half-hearted on that one.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:52.480
Wouldn’t want people to end up marmalised.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:56.950
1970, he oversaw the jamming of something.

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:57.950
’70?

00:03:57.950 --> 00:03:58.950
1970.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.840
- Is it pirate...
- Pirate radio!

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:05.120
Yes. I’ll give you the point there,
specifically what, where’s it coming from?

00:04:05.139 --> 00:04:06.139
- Luxembourg?
- North Sea?

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:10.720
You’re thinking Radio Luxembourg but no,
you’re absolutely right, it’s off in the North Sea.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.139
This was one of the Radio Carolines.

00:04:13.139 --> 00:04:14.380
- Oh!
- Was it?

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.160
You see I didn’t say that because
I thought it was a leading question

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:17.380
and it would be wrong.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:20.560
Yes, but this isn’t QI! You’re allowed
to go for that answer here.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:21.840
Awooga!

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:27.640
So he jammed Radio Caroline and part of the
response to that

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.880
was that Radio Caroline did what?

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:30.900
Jammed them right back.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:34.190
No, that actually would be... not illegal,
but it certainly would be, I think,

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.781
a declaration of war if you do that to a country.

00:04:36.781 --> 00:04:39.003
And when it comes to boats we’ve probably
got the bigger shooty ones in town.

00:04:39.003 --> 00:04:41.580
We might have slightly more.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:45.400
Because Caroline was on and off for a bit.
So was it off for a bit?

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:48.200
It was, they tried to overcome the jamming,
obviously,

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:50.780
what were they sending back over the airwaves?

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:54.010
Swear words. Filthy streams of invective…

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:55.770
They could have done, they were offshore.

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.800
Don’t forget Radio Caroline wasn’t illegal,

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.590
they just made it so that any British person
associating with it was breaking the law.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:04.160
“Vote for the other ones.”

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.210
Yes, absolutely right, they went political

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:10.180
and they started broadcasting pro-Conservative
propaganda back at them.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:13.670
Sorry! Pirate radio station broadcasting
pro-Conservative messages?

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:15.360
Let that sink in for a minute.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:18.440
“Vote Tory!”

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:20.780
Yeah, you wouldn’t get that
these days would you? Blimey.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:22.400
No, there’s no pirate radio stations!

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.020
- There are.
- Oh, there are.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:27.960
I was going to say, presumably you have deal
with those, Matt.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:31.800
Yes, they interfere over the broadcasts that
we are trying to make at work.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.350
And then we report them to OFCOM and then
they find them

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:36.160
and say no, you’re being naughty, please
don’t do that.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.051
When you said deal with them, that suggested
you somehow

00:05:38.051 --> 00:05:39.600
went round at night and dealt with them.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:40.730
With a baseball bat.

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:44.780
Matt Gray does have a black ski-mask and an
awful lot of black turtlenecks.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:48.860
A van just comes out of the OFCOM offices
with you guys in.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:55.260
John Stonehouse, still in charge of post and
telecommunications in the 1970s, introduced what?

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:56.840
It’s not stamps or anything because they’re
already there...

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:57.580
Oh, it is.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:59.200
- Is it stamps?
- Second-class...!

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:01.880
Is it second-class stamps?

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:05.340
Absolutely right, first and second-class stamps.
These all seem…

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:06.380
(You f***er!)

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:14.520
...fairly normal. In 1970 he was setting up
various companies, having things on the side.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:18.770
By 1974 most of these were in financial trouble.

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:20.180
What did he decide to do?

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:22.740
Print himself a whole book of stamps
as legal tender

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:26.060
and pay off his debts with
a massive box full of stamps.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:29.400
That would have been a better plan than the
one he actually had.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:35.240
Issue a single £150,000 stamp on the quiet
to a collector.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.440
Also a better plan than what he did.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.640
Jesus Christ! Rob a bank.

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:40.600
With stamps.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.380
Hang on, did he invent selfie stamps,

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.960
where you could send in a selfie and you’d
have your own stamp?

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:49.220
He didn’t set up his pirate radio station
did he, playing pro-Tory propaganda?

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:51.510
He’s in deep financial trouble here,

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:54.840
he’s doing creative accounting so he’s
going to be going to jail.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:56.800
Did he disappear?

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:59.400
Oh, a little bit more than that.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:00.400
Is he…?

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:01.400
Did he fake his death?

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:02.400
Yes, he did. He faked his…

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:04.620
Is he one of these clothes on the beach guys
and disappears?

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:08.660
Spot on. Exactly right, he faked his death,
November 1974,

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:11.070
leaving a pile of clothes on a Miami beach.

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:12.070
Whose clothes?

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:13.600
His clothes.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:15.230
Where was he actually going?

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:16.230
Cuba!

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:17.230
Australia.

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:18.230
That’s a long swim.

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:21.180
You’re one better than me, I was going to
say a long walk

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:22.250
which would have been quite stupid.

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:24.760
Well, swimming it naked,
he’s going to get cold.

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:27.380
He is. Maybe he slathered himself
in goose fat beforehand.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.360
And, hell, he’s going the wrong way round,
he should have gone from LA.

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:30.820
Yeah, the man’s a fool.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:34.820
Goose fat is what you use,
I’m right aren’t I?

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:36.580
That’s what you use for cross-channel swimming.

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:39.120
Yes, when you’re swimming from Miami to
Australia, that’s what you use.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:40.120
Goose fat, yes.

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:42.300
Most people use their arms and legs.
Gary, goose fat.

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:44.150
-“It’ll be fine!”
- Just bobs around...

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:45.430
“Gary, there’s a breaker.”

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:47.910
“It’s fine, the goose fat'll save me!”

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:52.070
Ejecting it from behind,
like propulsive goose fat.

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:54.350
Hey, a goose gives out a lot of fat, you know.

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:57.680
He’s just leaving this greasy trail on the
ocean as he goes.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:00.480
“Gary Brannan’s greasy ocean trail.”

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:01.480
Hell of a series.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:03.520
I once did a goose at Christmas
and it put out...

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:06.870
A single goose-- now in all fairness I got
it from a budget supermarket

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:10.070
so it may not have been the very best of geese,
let’s be honest.

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:11.110
Budget supermarkets sell geese?

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:12.110
For a tenner!

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:14.700
Yes, they just get them from the local pond,
and you know…

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:17.330
Ask no questions, tell no lies.
That’s the way I looked at it.

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:21.040
But I did, and it pumped half a litre of fat.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:25.370
It didn’t even drip out, it literally just
leaked fat.

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:27.720
You know those things when you get oil wells
and there’s a gusher…

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:30.990
“Goose fat, we’re rich! Ah, I'm greasy...”

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:35.470
“Quick take me to the Channel!
This is my only chance!”

00:08:35.470 --> 00:08:37.550
“White gold!”, he cried.

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:41.460
Did it deep fry itself? Half a litre?

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:43.620
It just... you put it on an angle to let it
all drain out

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:44.620
and it just kept coming and coming and coming.

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:49.430
It was a whole big mincemeat jar that big
was nearly full of goose fat.

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:52.600
No man in the world can eat that much 
roast potatoes, is what I’ve found.

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:54.100
Sorry, can I just point out:

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:57.520
mincemeat jar full of goose fat, that’s
quite a northern thing to say…

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:04.100
So yes, Stonehouse, meanwhile. 
Stonehouse was... I’m just trying to pull this back.

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.340
It was in my fridge for months!

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:09.140
I remember, you sent me a picture of it!

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:11.790
You actually sound closer to
tears than laughter, Gary.

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:16.010
Because, just after we’d had this massive
Christmas dinner where I’d done the goose.

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:20.110
I got all this goose fat, the holy grail of
Yorkshire puddings and roast potatoes...

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:22.540
Went on a f***ing diet
and I never touched the stuff.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:26.280
AUDIENCE: Aww.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:28.300
And in this jar was…

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:31.860
John Stonehouse…

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:34.050
Seamless(!)

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:36.060
John Stonehouse was en-route to Australia

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:39.080
setting up a new life with
his mistress and secretary.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:40.080
Are they two separate people?

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:43.960
Was he just being a real arse and taking his
mistress and his secretary?

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:48.030
Well, who was his new identity?
Whose identity had he taken on?

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:49.300
Did he nick a dead person's?

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:52.870
Yes, he did. Deceased husband of a constituent.

00:09:52.870 --> 00:09:57.700
So he deposited cash at one bank, picked it
up at another and the teller was suspicious

00:09:57.700 --> 00:09:59.620
and reported him to local police.

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:02.320
The police, when they interviewed him,

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:04.200
asked him to drop his trousers. Why?

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:05.200
Normal.

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.630
Was the person he was pretending to be a eunuch?

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:09.110
No, just his trousers, just his trousers.

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:13.140
Well they’re looking for an identifying mark,
are they not? So tattoo or birthmark.

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:16.820
Yes... neither. This is 1974.

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:17.820
Lord Lucan!

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:22.710
Yes. They suspected that he was Lord Lucan
who had very famously disappeared,

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:26.310
there’s a whole separate story there.

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:29.880
Someone suspiciously turning up
with an English accent in Australia,

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:31.080
depositing large sums of money.

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.410
Who is obviously on the run...

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:36.260
Lord Lucan had a large scar down his leg.

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:38.400
So the police were going,
well, drop your trousers,

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:40.779
we want to see if you’re Lord Lucan.
He wasn't...

00:10:40.779 --> 00:10:42.770
Did he debag himself in aid of the…

00:10:42.770 --> 00:10:46.150
Because if he did, and he dropped his trousers
and there’s no scar,

00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:47.500
he should surely be let go.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:50.090
Yes, and then they’re not going to be suspicious
of him ever again.

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:52.580
Yes, he was still arrested, they just knew
he wasn’t Lord Lucan.

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:53.580
Oh.

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:56.840
“We know you’re somebody but we don’t
know who.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:59.430
“Standard procedure, drop the trousers.”

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:02.920
That was essentially... yeah, that would have
been 1974.

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:07.770
“What do we do second?”
“I don’t know, it’s never failed.”

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:09.780
“Every arse tells a story.”

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:16.240
“All right, we’re going to need
to get your arse print here,

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:20.000
“please just sit in this ink for a little
while, and then on this paper...”

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:22.820
“Just reverse onto this paper.”

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:25.440
“It’s thumbprint. Thumbprint!”

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:29.930
He’s arrested, six months later he’s deported
to the UK.

00:11:29.930 --> 00:11:34.290
He’s remanded in Brixton prison. What has
he not done at any point during this?

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:35.870
Pulled his trousers up.

